<HowtoLayout slug="product-photo-to-lifestyle-image" ctaLabel="Create your account" faq={faq}>
  <p class="standfirst">The scene is the easy part. What breaks is the product: a label that comes back misspelled, a cap that changed shape, an amber bottle gone orange. This is the method that keeps it true.</p>

  <h2>How do you turn a product photo into a lifestyle image?</h2>

  <p>Treat the packshot as the reference every result is judged against. Pick the scene for the place it will run, list what may not change, and write an edit brief that names it. Run that brief on several engines, fix framing in an editor, then check each result against the packshot. hubStudio does all of it in one app.</p>

  <p>Here is the sequence, start to finish:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Pick the scene for a slot.</strong> Decide where the picture will run before you decide what goes in it, because the slot sets the rules.</li>
    <li><strong>Lock the product.</strong> Shape, label, color, finish, count, scale: write them down.</li>
    <li><strong>Prepare the source.</strong> A sharp packshot with the whole product in frame, plus a close crop of the label when the engine takes a second picture.</li>
    <li><strong>Write the brief.</strong> Scene, light and placement, then one full sentence on what stays.</li>
    <li><strong>Run it on several engines.</strong> Same brief, same source.</li>
    <li><strong>Fix what needs no new run.</strong> Crop, light and format belong in the Image editor; anything on the product itself means another run.</li>
    <li><strong>Check it against the packshot</strong>, line by line, before anyone approves it.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What must stay fixed, and what can the scene change?</h2>

  <p>Everything a buyer will hold in their hand stays as it was shot. Everything around it is yours to change.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">What it covers</th>
          <th scope="col">Keep fixed</th>
          <th scope="col">The scene may change</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Shape</th>
          <td>Silhouette, cap, edges, proportions</td>
          <td>Nothing on the product itself</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Label and print</th>
          <td>Every word, its order, typeface, size and position</td>
          <td>Nothing: a garbled label is a rerun</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Color and finish</th>
          <td>Product color, glass tint, matte or gloss</td>
          <td>Only the warmth of the light, and only a touch</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Logo and marks</th>
          <td>One logo, in its place, never mirrored</td>
          <td>No new marks on props or background</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Count and parts</th>
          <td>One unit, only the parts that ship with it</td>
          <td>Props that clearly are not the product</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Scale</th>
          <td>Real size against hands, shelves, towels</td>
          <td>Camera distance and crop</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Setting</th>
          <td>Nothing</td>
          <td>Surface, room, props, season, time of day</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">Light</th>
          <td>One source, one shadow, the same on product and set</td>
          <td>Direction, warmth and softness</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>The angle is the trap. An engine can only show sides of the product that the packshot shows. Ask for the back of a box shot from the front and it will invent one.</p>

  <h2>Where will the lifestyle image run?</h2>

  <p>Settle this before the brief, because the destination decides what the file may carry. A marketplace main image and a social post live under different rules.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Amazon's product image guide requires a pure white background on the main image and lets only a limited number of product types use a lifestyle image there; scenes of the product in use go in the additional image slots, coded PT01 to PT99.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central, Product image guide, US store, page undated, read October 8, 2026.</p>

  <p>The same guide says every product image must accurately represent the product being sold and match its title. A generated scene is held to that line like any photograph.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google Merchant Center takes up to five lifestyle images per product, at least 600 by 600 pixels, with no promotional text, watermark, logo overlay or border, and requires images made with generative AI to keep metadata saying so, such as the IPTC DigitalSourceType tag.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Merchant Center Help, lifestyle image link attribute, page undated, read October 8, 2026.</p>

