  <h2>What makes a packshot a good source?</h2>

  <p>Sharp focus, the whole product in frame, even light, and a label you can read at full size. If you can't read it on the packshot, the engine can't copy it.</p>

  <p>Then mind the size. The Image studio resizes source pictures in your browser to 1,536 pixels on the long side before sending them, which also removes the original file's metadata. Small print on a wide packshot loses detail at that size. Crop the packshot close to the product, and on an engine that takes several source pictures, add a tight crop of the label as the second one.</p>

  <p>Pick the job <strong>Edit an image</strong>. ChatGPT Image 2, Nano Banana 2 and Pro, and Seedream 4.5 and 5.0 Pro take up to four source pictures. FLUX.1 Kontext Pro and Max take one. The shape of the result follows the first picture you upload, so put the packshot first and change <strong>Shape</strong> if the slot needs another. <a href="/help/create-an-image">The Create an image help article</a> lists every option per engine.</p>

  <h2>How do you write the brief?</h2>

  <p>Write it as an edit instruction, not a description of a new picture. Name the scene and the light, and say where the product sits. Then give a full sentence to what stays. One engine maker spells this out.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google's image generation documentation tells users that to preserve critical details such as a logo during an edit, they should "describe them in great detail along with your edit request."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Gemini API image generation documentation, the maker's own page, last updated October 6, 2026.</p>

  <p>A working example, for a serum bottle shot on white:</p>

  <div class="prompt">
    <p class="prompt__label">Prompt example</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">Using the provided images, place the bottle from image 1 on a pale limestone bathroom shelf beside a folded linen towel. Early morning window light from the left, one soft shadow falling to the right.</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">Keep the bottle exactly as it is in image 1: amber glass, black dropper cap, the same proportions. The white label reads "DAILY SERUM" above "30 ml", same typeface, size and position; use image 2 for its detail. No other text, no logos, no second bottle.</p>
    <p class="prompt__body">The bottle fills about a third of the frame height, lower right, shelf edge running out of frame. Portrait 4:5, real photograph, no glossy retouching.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Put the label's words in quotation marks. Black Forest Labs, which makes the FLUX.1 Kontext engines, says in its own editing guide that quotation marks around the specific text are the most effective way to edit text in a picture.</p>

  <h2>Where do the Lifestyle product scene skill and Improve with AI fit?</h2>

  <p>They save you from writing the same rules into every brief. Open <strong>Skills</strong>, then <strong>Catalog</strong>, find <strong>Lifestyle product scene</strong>, read it with <strong>View details</strong> and click <strong>Add to my skills</strong>. From then on, every skill switched on for <strong>Images and video</strong> rides along when <strong>Improve with AI</strong> rewrites a prompt.</p>

  <p>Improve with AI rewrites what you typed for the engine you picked. It knows the job, so an edit comes back as an edit instruction. It never changes your settings. Under the box you get <strong>Prompt rewritten</strong>, a short list of what changed, and <strong>Undo</strong>. Each rewrite is a small paid call against your prepaid balance.</p>

  <p>Read the rewrite before you run it. If your keep sentence got shorter, put it back.</p>

  <h2>Why run the same brief on several engines?</h2>

  <p>Because they fail differently, and you won't know which one holds your label until you see it. Each run opens its own tab, newest first, and you can start the next run while the first renders. The price shows before each run; a run that fails is not charged.</p>

