  <p>On the ChatGPT Image engines, ask for several images in one run (one to ten) and keep the best. You pay for the images that come back.</p>

  <p>No engine guarantees the label, and its makers say as much.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">OpenAI lists text rendering among its image model's limitations and says the model "may occasionally struggle to maintain visual consistency for recurring characters or brand elements across multiple generations."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: OpenAI image generation guide, the maker's own documentation, page undated, read October 8, 2026.</p>

  <h2>What can you fix in the editor, and what needs a new run?</h2>

  <p>Click <strong>Edit</strong> on a ready picture and the Image editor opens. It is free and runs in your browser. Use it for what sits around the product: crop to a network format in <strong>Social</strong> or <strong>Crop</strong>, adjust light and color, then save a copy to the Assets Library next to the render.</p>

  <p>Go easy in <strong>Adjust</strong>. Warmth and saturation move the whole picture, product included, so check the label color again afterwards.</p>

  <p>Anything on the product itself needs a new run with the <strong>Edit an image</strong> job: a wrong letter, a changed cap, a second logo. On the ChatGPT Image engines you can add a <strong>Mask</strong>, a PNG whose transparent areas mark what may change in the first picture. Leave the product solid and the background transparent. Then check the product's edge anyway.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">OpenAI's guide says the model "uses the mask as guidance, but may not follow its exact shape with complete precision."</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: OpenAI image generation guide, page undated, read October 8, 2026.</p>

  <h2>How do you check product fidelity before it ships?</h2>

  <p>Open the result and the packshot side by side at the same size, then go down the list. A failure on shape or label sends the picture back for a rerun. Do not letter a label back in by hand: you would be drawing the product, not showing it.</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Silhouette.</strong> Same outline, cap, edges and proportions.</li>
    <li><strong>Label.</strong> Every word spelled as printed, in the same order, typeface and position. Nothing added.</li>
    <li><strong>Color.</strong> Product and finish match the packshot on the same screen, after any edit in Adjust.</li>
    <li><strong>Logo.</strong> Once, in its place, not mirrored, not repeated on a prop.</li>
    <li><strong>Count.</strong> One unit, and only the parts that ship with it.</li>
    <li><strong>Scale.</strong> The size reads right against the towel, the hand, the shelf.</li>
    <li><strong>Light.</strong> One direction. The shadow falls away from it, and glass reflects the room it sits in.</li>
    <li><strong>Edges.</strong> No halo or cut-out line where the product meets the scene.</li>
    <li><strong>People.</strong> If the scene shows a person, count the fingers. On Amazon, a photorealistic person made entirely by AI needs a tag.</li>
    <li><strong>File.</strong> For Google's lifestyle slot, no text or logo overlay, and the AI metadata still in the file.</li>
  </ul>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Amazon asks sellers to add the keyword contains-synthetic-performer to the dc:subject (XMP) field when an image shows photorealistic people entirely generated by AI, and not when the image shows no people.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Amazon Seller Central, Product image guide, US store, page undated, read October 8, 2026.</p>

  <p>To see what a file carries, drop it in the Image anonymizer: it lists the hidden data first. For a Google lifestyle image, stop there. The clean copy it offers is exactly what that slot does not want.</p>

  <p>When the list passes, click <strong>Send for validation</strong> on the tab. The render goes to a teammate or a client as version 1 of an approval thread. Every render also stays in History with its prompt and engine, so the next product in the range starts from <strong>Reuse prompt</strong>, not from a blank box.</p>

  <h2>When should you photograph the scene instead?</h2>

  <p>When the picture is the claim. A texture close-up, the drape of a fabric, a cream's finish on skin: those prove something about the product, and generation has nothing real to prove it with. The <a href="/resources/insights/shoot-it-or-generate-it">Shoot it or generate it</a> insight sets the rule asset by asset. Shoot the anchor; generate the scenes that repeat.</p>

  <p>If you'd rather hand over the whole listing set, shot and generated, <a href="/services/design/ecommerce">the ecommerce design service</a> and <a href="/solutions/ai-production/image">AI image production</a> pages describe how the studio takes it on. To run the method yourself, <a href="/app/create">the create page of the app</a> shows the Image studio and the Skills catalog.</p>

  <h2>FAQ</h2>

